<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Powhatan Confederacy</h1><p class="st-meta">1570 CE – 1677 CE (approx.) · capital Werowocomoco</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The paramount chiefdom of Tsenacommacah in coastal Virginia, whose ruler Powhatan faced the English settlers of Jamestown.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In the late 16th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wahunsenacah">Wahunsenacah</a>, known to the English as Powhatan, inherited six chiefdoms on the upper James and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york-maine">York</a> rivers. Through conquest and pressure he expanded his rule to some 30 Algonquian-speaking peoples between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potomac-river">Potomac</a> and the James, perhaps 15,000 people, governing from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/werowocomoco">Werowocomoco</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york-river-virginia">York River</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Each subject people kept its own weroance, or chief, who paid tribute in corn, deerskins, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and pearls to the paramount ruler. Priests held great influence, and towns of bark-covered houses stood among fields of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, beans and squash. When the English founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamestown">Jamestown</a> in 1607, Powhatan hoped to make them tributaries; relations swung between trade and war, and his daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pocahontas">Pocahontas's</a> marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-rolfe">John Rolfe</a> in 1614 brought a brief peace.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>His brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opechancanough">Opechancanough</a> launched great attacks on the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-virginia">Colony of Virginia</a> in 1622 and 1644; captured and killed in 1646, he left the chiefdom tributary to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-middle-plantation">Treaty of Middle Plantation</a> in 1677, signed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cockacoeske">Cockacoeske</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamunkey">Pamunkey</a>, ended it, though Pamunkey and Mattaponi reservations survive.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/powhatan-confederacy.jpg" alt="Powhatan Confederacy" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: Joshua Sarquilla · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tsenacommacah_(orthographic_projection).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1570 CE – 1677 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Werowocomoco</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Wahunsenacah, Opechancanough, Cockacoeske</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Powhatan traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Powhatan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-states">United States</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tsenacommacah, Powhatan Chiefdom</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1624&amp;focus=powhatan-confederacy" aria-label="Powhatan Confederacy on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2763 831 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Powhatan Confederacy lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3083 1087L3080 1092"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3080 1092h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3083 1087h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3083 1087h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Werowocomoco. <a href="/atlas?year=1624&amp;focus=powhatan-confederacy">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

